Clean Code & Loops enumerate() instead of range(len()): 

Get both the index and value at the same time without tracking lengths manually.

zip() for parallel loops: Loop over two or more lists at the same time cleanly.

List and Dict Comprehensions: Replace multi-line filter-and-append loops with fast, readable single-line expressions.

The Walrus Operator (:=): Assign values inside a condition or loop to avoid running the same expression twice.



# List comprehension
squares = [x**2 for x in range(10)]

# Dictionary comprehension
name_lengths = {name: len(name) for name in ["Alice", "Bob"]}

#Iterate with enumerate(): Get both the item index and the value simultaneously without manual counters.

for index, value in enumerate(["a", "b", "c"]):
    print(index, value)

#Zip Multiple Iterables: Loop through two or more lists at the same time in parallel.
names = ["Alice", "Bob"]
scores = [85, 92]
for name, score in zip(names, scores):
    print(f"{name}: {score}")


